### What is the Accounts Merge problem?

Given a list of accounts where each account is a name followed by one or more emails, merge accounts that share any email. Return merged accounts with the name and sorted unique emails.

At the National Bank of India, a single customer like Rajesh may have registered multiple profiles that share a verified email. The master data system needs one golden record per real person.

```java
package com.bank.identity;

import java.util.*;

public class AccountsMergeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<List<String>> accounts = List.of(
            List.of("Rajesh", "rajesh@bank.in", "rajesh@gmail.com"),
            List.of("Priya", "priya@bank.in"),
            List.of("Rajesh", "rajesh@work.in", "rajesh@gmail.com")
        );
        List<List<String>> merged = accountsMerge(accounts);
        merged.forEach(System.out::println);
    }

    // implementation below
}
```

```text
[Rajesh, rajesh@bank.in, rajesh@gmail.com, rajesh@work.in]
[Priya, priya@bank.in]
```

### How does Union-Find solve this?

Assign each first-seen email a unique integer ID. For each account row, union the first email's ID with every other email's ID. This makes all emails within one account share a root. Because union is transitive, two accounts that share an email will have their emails linked into the same set.

```java
package com.bank.identity;

import java.util.*;

public class AccountsMerge {

    static int[] parent, rank;

    static int find(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) parent[x] = find(parent[x]);
        return parent[x];
    }

    static boolean union(int a, int b) {
        int ra = find(a), rb = find(b);
        if (ra == rb) return false;
        if (rank[ra] < rank[rb]) { int t = ra; ra = rb; rb = t; }
        parent[rb] = ra;
        if (rank[ra] == rank[rb]) rank[ra]++;
        return true;
    }

    static List<List<String>> accountsMerge(List<List<String>> accounts) {
        Map<String, Integer> emailToId = new HashMap<>();
        Map<String, String> emailToName = new HashMap<>();

        // Step 1: assign IDs
        for (List<String> acct : accounts) {
            String name = acct.get(0);
            for (int i = 1; i < acct.size(); i++) {
                String email = acct.get(i);
                emailToId.putIfAbsent(email, emailToId.size());
                emailToName.put(email, name);
            }
        }

        // Step 2: init Union-Find
        int n = emailToId.size();
        parent = new int[n];
        rank = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;

        // Step 3: union emails within each account
        for (List<String> acct : accounts) {
            int firstId = emailToId.get(acct.get(1));
            for (int i = 2; i < acct.size(); i++) {
                union(firstId, emailToId.get(acct.get(i)));
            }
        }

        // Step 4: group by root
        Map<Integer, List<String>> groups = new HashMap<>();
        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : emailToId.entrySet()) {
            int root = find(entry.getValue());
            groups.computeIfAbsent(root, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(entry.getKey());
        }

        // Step 5: build result
        List<List<String>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        for (List<String> emails : groups.values()) {
            Collections.sort(emails);
            String name = emailToName.get(emails.get(0));
            List<String> merged = new ArrayList<>();
            merged.add(name);
            merged.addAll(emails);
            result.add(merged);
        }
        return result;
    }
}
```

### Why are emails the identity key, not names?

Two different customers can have the same name, but a verified email belongs to exactly one person. Unioning on names would incorrectly merge distinct people who happen to share a name.

```java
// WRONG: merging by name
// "Rajesh" with rajesh@bank.in
// "Rajesh" with rajesh@other.in  (different person!)
// Merging by name would fuse them incorrectly.

// CORRECT: merging by shared email
// Only accounts that share at least one email are the same person.
```

### How does transitivity work?

If account A has emails {e1, e2} and account B has emails {e2, e3}, they share e2 so they merge into {e1, e2, e3}. If account C has {e3, e4}, it shares e3 with the merged set, so all three become {e1, e2, e3, e4}.

```java
package com.bank.identity;

import java.util.List;

public class TransitiveExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<List<String>> accounts = List.of(
            List.of("Rajesh", "e1@bank.in", "e2@bank.in"),
            List.of("Rajesh", "e2@bank.in", "e3@bank.in"),
            List.of("Rajesh", "e3@bank.in", "e4@bank.in")
        );
        // After union: e1, e2, e3, e4 all share one root
        // Result: [Rajesh, e1@bank.in, e2@bank.in, e3@bank.in, e4@bank.in]
        AccountsMerge.accountsMerge(accounts).forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

```text
[Rajesh, e1@bank.in, e2@bank.in, e3@bank.in, e4@bank.in]
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

The time is dominated by sorting the emails within each group, which is O(E log E) where E is the total number of emails. The union operations with path compression and rank are nearly constant, O(alpha(E)) per operation.

| Step | Time |
|------|------|
| Assign IDs | O(E) |
| Union | O(E * alpha(E)) |
| Group by root | O(E) |
| Sort groups | O(E log E) |
| Total | O(E log E) |

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include accounts with only one email (nothing to union), all accounts being the same person, and no shared emails at all.

```java
// Edge: all separate
List.of(
    List.of("A", "a@x.com"),
    List.of("B", "b@x.com"),
    List.of("C", "c@x.com")
);
// Result: three separate accounts

// Edge: all one person
List.of(
    List.of("A", "a@x.com", "b@x.com"),
    List.of("A", "b@x.com", "c@x.com")
);
// Result: one account with a, b, c
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is merging by name instead of by shared email. Another is forgetting to sort the emails in the output.

```java
// WRONG: using name as the merge key
Map<String, Set<String>> byName = new HashMap<>();
for (List<String> acct : accounts) {
    byName.computeIfAbsent(acct.get(0), k -> new HashSet<>())
          .addAll(acct.subList(1, acct.size()));
}
// This merges ALL accounts with the same name, even if they are different people!

// CORRECT: use Union-Find on emails
// Only accounts sharing an email get merged
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Path compression + union by rank.** Together these make Union-Find nearly O(1) per operation. Always implement both for interview-grade code.
- **DFS alternative.** Build a graph where each email is a node and edges connect emails within the same account. Run DFS/BFS from each unvisited email to find connected components. Same result, different data structure.
- **Production deduplication.** In real master data management, email matching is one signal among many (phone, address, name similarity). But the Union-Find pattern remains the core merge engine.
- **Idempotency.** Running the merge multiple times on the same input produces the same result, which is important for batch pipelines that may retry.
